# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. HOW TO PURIFY TAP WATER
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
dict(
    slug="how-to-purify-tap-water",
    title="How to Purify Tap Water: Every Method Compared",
    meta_description="Boiling, filtration, UV, chemical tablets, and SODIS compared: what each method removes, what it misses, and which to use where.",
    intro="The most reliable way to purify tap water is a rolling boil for one minute (three minutes above 2,000 meters), which kills bacteria, viruses, and protozoa. Filtration, UV light, chemical tablets, and solar disinfection each work too &mdash; but every method has blind spots, and the right choice depends on what you're trying to remove.",
    sections=[
        ("Boiling: The Gold Standard for Pathogens", """<p>Boiling is the oldest purification method and still the most dependable against microorganisms. The CDC recommends bringing water to a full rolling boil for <strong>one minute</strong> &mdash; and <strong>three minutes at elevations above 2,000 meters (about 6,500 feet)</strong>, where water boils at a lower temperature. That treatment reliably kills bacteria such as <em>E. coli</em> and <em>Salmonella</em>, viruses like norovirus and hepatitis A, and hardy protozoa including <em>Giardia</em> and <em>Cryptosporidium</em>, which resist chlorine.</p><p>What boiling does <em>not</em> do is remove anything that isn't alive. Heavy metals like lead and arsenic, nitrates, pesticides, and salts all stay in the water &mdash; in fact, boiling slightly concentrates them as water evaporates. If your concern is chemical contamination rather than germs, boiling is the wrong tool. Let boiled water cool covered, and store it in a clean, closed container.</p>"""),
        ("Filtration: Know What Your Filter Actually Removes", """<p>Filters vary enormously, and the pore size and media determine what they catch.</p><ul class="space-y-2"><li><strong>Activated carbon</strong> (pitcher and faucet filters) improves taste and removes chlorine, many pesticides, and some organic chemicals. Standard carbon filters do <em>not</em> reliably remove bacteria, viruses, or dissolved metals unless certified for them.</li><li><strong>Ceramic filters</strong> physically block bacteria and protozoa down to roughly 0.2&ndash;0.5 microns, but viruses (around 0.02&ndash;0.3 microns) can slip through.</li><li><strong>Hollow fiber membranes</strong>, common in travel filters, remove bacteria and protozoa; only the tightest (0.02 micron) versions also catch viruses.</li><li><strong>Reverse osmosis (RO)</strong> forces water through a semi-permeable membrane and removes nearly everything: pathogens, lead, arsenic, nitrates, fluoride, and most dissolved solids. It is the most complete treatment available for home use, at the cost of wasted water and slower flow.</li></ul><p>Whatever you buy, check for NSF/ANSI certification (Standard 53 for health contaminants, Standard 58 for RO, P231 for microbiological purifiers) rather than trusting marketing claims.</p>"""),
        ("UV Light: Effective, With Caveats", """<p>Ultraviolet (UV-C) treatment scrambles the DNA of bacteria, viruses, and protozoa so they can't reproduce. Home UV systems and portable UV pens both work well against all three pathogen classes &mdash; including chlorine-resistant <em>Cryptosporidium</em> &mdash; which makes UV one of the few methods that matches boiling on microbes.</p><p>The caveats: UV only works in <strong>clear water</strong>, because particles shield microorganisms from the light. Cloudy water must be pre-filtered. UV also removes nothing chemical &mdash; no metals, no salts, no pesticides &mdash; and battery-powered pens can fail without warning, so travelers should carry a chemical backup. See our <a href="/guides/best-travel-water-filters/" class="text-sky-700 hover:underline">travel filter and purifier guide</a> for how UV fits into a travel kit.</p>"""),
        ("Chemical Purification: Chlorine Dioxide and Iodine", """<p>Purification tablets and drops are cheap, weightless, and never break &mdash; which is why they're the standard backup method. <strong>Chlorine dioxide</strong> is the better modern choice: it kills bacteria and viruses within about 30 minutes and, unlike plain chlorine or iodine, will also inactivate <em>Cryptosporidium</em> if you wait the full contact time (up to 4 hours for cold or dirty water).</p><p><strong>Iodine</strong> tablets work against bacteria, viruses, and <em>Giardia</em>, but not <em>Cryptosporidium</em>, and they leave a medicinal taste. Iodine is not recommended for pregnant women, people with thyroid conditions, or continuous use beyond a few weeks. With either chemical, cold water needs a longer contact time, and neither removes chemical contaminants &mdash; they only address the biological side.</p>"""),
        ("SODIS: Solar Disinfection When You Have Nothing Else", """<p>Solar disinfection (SODIS) is a WHO-recognized last-resort method used widely in low-income regions. Fill a clear PET plastic bottle (2 liters or smaller) with clear water, and lay it in full, direct sunlight for <strong>at least 6 hours</strong> &mdash; or two full days if the sky is more than half cloudy. The combination of UV-A radiation and heat inactivates most bacteria, viruses, and protozoa.</p><p>SODIS costs nothing and requires no equipment beyond a bottle, but it is slow, weather-dependent, works only with clear water, and provides no chemical removal. Treat it as an emergency technique, not a plan.</p>"""),
        ("Which Method Should You Use?", """<p>Match the method to the threat and the setting:</p><ul class="space-y-2"><li><strong>Travel in a country rated Caution or Not Safe</strong> (check the <a href="/map/" class="text-sky-700 hover:underline">world safety map</a>): a purifier bottle or boiling for drinking water; bottled water as the convenient default.</li><li><strong>Microbial worry at home</strong> (boil water advisory, well contamination): boiling first, then a certified purifier &mdash; see our <a href="/guides/boil-water-advisory-guide/" class="text-sky-700 hover:underline">boil water advisory guide</a>.</li><li><strong>Chemical worry at home</strong> (lead, nitrates, arsenic, PFAS): reverse osmosis or a carbon filter specifically certified for that contaminant. Boiling and UV do nothing here.</li><li><strong>Taste and chlorine smell</strong>: a simple activated carbon pitcher is enough.</li><li><strong>Backcountry or emergencies</strong>: hollow fiber filter plus chlorine dioxide tablets covers essentially everything biological.</li></ul>"""),
    ],
    faqs=[
        ("How long should I boil tap water to make it safe?", "Bring it to a full rolling boil for 1 minute at low elevations, or 3 minutes above 2,000 meters (6,500 feet), per CDC guidance. Longer boiling adds no safety benefit and just concentrates any dissolved minerals."),
        ("Does boiling water remove lead or other metals?", "No. Boiling kills microorganisms but leaves lead, arsenic, nitrates, and other dissolved chemicals behind &mdash; and slightly concentrates them as water evaporates. Use a certified filter or reverse osmosis for metals."),
        ("Do water filter pitchers make unsafe water safe to drink?", "Usually not. Standard carbon pitchers improve taste and remove chlorine and some chemicals, but most are not certified to remove bacteria or viruses. In a country with unsafe tap water, a pitcher filter is not sufficient protection."),
        ("What kills Cryptosporidium in water?", "Boiling, reverse osmosis, filters rated to 1 micron absolute or better, UV treatment of clear water, and chlorine dioxide with an extended contact time. Plain chlorine bleach and iodine do not reliably kill Cryptosporidium."),
        ("Is purified water the same as distilled water?", "No. Purified is a general term for water treated to remove contaminants by any method. Distilled water is specifically boiled into steam and recondensed, which removes minerals and most contaminants but is energy-intensive and flat-tasting."),
    ],
    related=["best-travel-water-filters", "boil-water-advisory-guide", "tds-in-drinking-water"],
),

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. IS ICE SAFE WHEN TRAVELING
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
dict(
    slug="is-ice-safe-when-traveling",
    title="Is Ice Safe in Drinks When Traveling?",
    meta_description="Ice carries the same risk as the tap water it was made from &mdash; freezing kills nothing. How to judge ice safety by venue and destination.",
    intro="Ice is only as safe as the water it was frozen from &mdash; and freezing does not kill the bacteria, viruses, or parasites that cause traveler's diarrhea. In countries where you wouldn't drink the tap water, you shouldn't assume the ice is safe either, though commercially produced ice in reputable venues is often a reasonable exception.",
    sections=[
        ("Freezing Does Not Kill Pathogens", """<p>This is the single most important fact about ice: <strong>freezing preserves microorganisms rather than destroying them</strong>. <em>E. coli</em>, <em>Salmonella</em>, norovirus, hepatitis A, <em>Giardia</em>, and <em>Cryptosporidium</em> all survive freezing readily &mdash; laboratories literally use freezers to store microbe cultures long-term. When the cube melts into your drink, whatever was in the source water is released, fully viable.</p><p>Alcohol doesn't rescue you either. The concentration of alcohol in a cocktail or a whisky on the rocks is nowhere near enough to disinfect contaminated meltwater. Nor does the cold itself &mdash; pathogens simply wait.</p>"""),
        ("Ice Inherits the Risk of Its Source Water", """<p>The question &ldquo;is the ice safe?&rdquo; always reduces to &ldquo;what water was it made from?&rdquo; In destinations rated <strong>Safe</strong> or <strong>Generally Safe</strong> on our <a href="/map/" class="text-sky-700 hover:underline">world map</a>, ice made from the tap carries the same negligible risk as the tap itself &mdash; drink freely. In countries rated <strong>Caution</strong> or <strong>Not Safe</strong>, such as <a href="/country/mexico/" class="text-sky-700 hover:underline">Mexico</a>, <a href="/country/india/" class="text-sky-700 hover:underline">India</a>, or <a href="/country/egypt/" class="text-sky-700 hover:underline">Egypt</a>, homemade ice from the tap is exactly as risky as drinking the tap water directly.</p><p>The wrinkle is that in many of those same countries, most ice served commercially is <em>not</em> made from tap water. Because locals also avoid the tap, a large purified-ice industry exists to supply bars, restaurants, and street vendors.</p>"""),
        ("The Cylindrical Hole: Reading Factory Ice", """<p>Commercial ice plants in Latin America, Southeast Asia, and elsewhere typically produce ice from purified water in standardized machines &mdash; and their cubes have a recognizable shape: <strong>uniform cylinders with a round hole through the middle</strong>, or perfectly regular shapes with a dimple. If the ice in your drink is a neat tube with a hole, it very likely came from a commercial plant using treated water.</p><p>By contrast, irregular chunks, crushed ice of unknown origin, or cubes clearly cracked from a homemade block deserve more suspicion &mdash; block ice in markets is sometimes made for keeping produce and fish cold, not for consumption. Shape is not a guarantee, but it is genuinely useful evidence, and it's the same heuristic experienced expats use.</p>"""),
        ("Hotels and Restaurants vs. Street Vendors", """<p>Context matters as much as shape. Established hotels and tourist-oriented restaurants in Caution-rated countries almost universally buy purified ice or make it from filtered water &mdash; serving contaminated ice to guests is terrible for business, and many hotel chains audit this. Asking &ldquo;is the ice made from purified water?&rdquo; is a normal question that staff answer routinely. See our <a href="/guides/hotel-tap-water-safety/" class="text-sky-700 hover:underline">hotel tap water guide</a> for the fuller picture on hotel water systems.</p><p>Street vendors are the genuinely mixed case. Many buy bagged purified ice; some use block ice of unknown origin, crushed on the spot with tools and surfaces that introduce their own contamination. In a Not Safe country, skipping ice from street stalls &mdash; especially crushed ice in fresh juices and shaved-ice desserts &mdash; removes one of the more plausible infection routes while costing you little.</p>"""),
        ("Practical Rules by Destination Rating", """<ul class="space-y-2"><li><strong>Safe / Generally Safe countries:</strong> ice is a non-issue. Enjoy it.</li><li><strong>Caution countries:</strong> ice in hotels, proper restaurants, and chain cafes is generally fine, particularly cylindrical factory ice. Be choosier at street level.</li><li><strong>Not Safe countries:</strong> stick to venues that can tell you their ice is purified, favor factory-shaped cubes, and skip ice in street drinks. When in doubt, order drinks bottled and cold instead &mdash; a refrigerator achieves what ice does without the risk.</li></ul><p>And if you do get it wrong occasionally, remember that dose matters: a single small cube in one drink is a smaller exposure than a glass of tap water. Most traveler's diarrhea resolves on its own, but the easiest illness to treat is the one you avoided.</p>"""),
    ],
    faqs=[
        ("Does freezing water kill bacteria and viruses?", "No. Freezing preserves pathogens rather than killing them. Bacteria, viruses, and parasites survive in ice and become active again as it melts into your drink."),
        ("Does alcohol in a drink make contaminated ice safe?", "No. The alcohol concentration in cocktails, beer, or even spirits on the rocks is far too low to disinfect meltwater from contaminated ice."),
        ("How can I tell if ice was commercially produced?", "Commercial factory ice is typically a uniform cylinder with a round hole through the center, or perfectly regular cubes. Irregular chunks broken from a block are more likely homemade or intended for cooling goods, not consumption."),
        ("Is ice safe in all-inclusive resorts in Mexico or the Caribbean?", "Almost always yes. Large resorts make ice from purified water or buy commercial purified ice, since guest illness is a serious liability. The bigger ice risk is at street level, not in resorts."),
        ("Should I avoid frozen drinks like smoothies and slushies when traveling?", "In Caution and Not Safe countries, blended ice drinks from street vendors are among the riskier options, since crushed ice of unknown origin mixes thoroughly into the drink. From reputable cafes and hotels they are generally fine."),
    ],
    related=["hotel-tap-water-safety", "brushing-teeth-tap-water-abroad", "how-to-purify-tap-water"],
),

